# Code Style Guidelines

## TypeScript

- Use strict TypeScript configuration
- Prefer interfaces over types
- Use descriptive names with auxiliary verbs (isLoading, hasError)
- Define types in separate `.types.ts` files within entities
- Use generic types when appropriate
- Avoid `any` type, use `unknown` if needed

## React Components

- Use functional components with TypeScript interfaces
- Use functions declarations: `function Component() {}`
- Event handlers should have "handle" prefix: `handleClick`, `handleKeyDown`
- Use early returns for better readability
- Implement accessibility features (tabindex, aria-label, etc.)
- Use React.memo for expensive components
- Use proper key props for lists

## CSS Modules

- Use `.module.css` extension
- Use clsx for conditional classes instead of ternary operators
- Keep styles scoped to components
- Use CSS custom properties for theming

## State Management

- Use Zustand for global state
- Use React Query for server state
- Use React Hook Form for form state
- Keep state as close to where it's used as possible

## Function Exports

- Always use **separate functions** instead of objects with methods
- Export individual functions: `export function functionName() {}`
- Avoid object-based patterns: `export const someApi = { method: () => {} }`
- Follow functional programming principles
- Each function should be independently importable
- Apply this to API functions, utilities, helpers, services, and any other function collections

```typescript
// ✅ Good - separate functions (API example)
export function getUsers() {
  return apiClient.get<User[]>('/users', { schema: UserListSchema });
}

export function getUser(id: string) {
  return apiClient.get<User>(`/users/${id}`, { schema: UserSchema });
}

// ✅ Good - separate functions (utils example)
export function formatDate(date: Date) {
  return dayjs(date).format('DD.MM.YYYY');
}

export function validateEmail(email: string) {
  return /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email);
}

// ❌ Bad - object with methods
export const userApi = {
  getUsers: () => apiClient.get<User[]>('/users', { schema: UserListSchema }),
  getUser: (id: string) => apiClient.get<User>(`/users/${id}`, { schema: UserSchema }),
};

export const utils = {
  formatDate: (date: Date) => dayjs(date).format('DD.MM.YYYY'),
  validateEmail: (email: string) => /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email),
};
```

## Error Handling

- Use try-catch blocks for async operations
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Show user-friendly error messages
- Log errors for debugging

## Performance

- Use useMemo for expensive calculations
- Use useCallback for function props
- Implement proper loading states
- Use React.lazy for code splitting

## Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML elements
- Implement proper ARIA attributes
- Ensure keyboard navigation
- Provide alternative text for images
- Test with screen readers

## Comments and Documentation

- **Always use English** for all comments, JSDoc documentation, and inline code explanations
- Write clear and descriptive comments explaining the "why", not just the "what"
- Use JSDoc format for function and component documentation
- Include parameter types and return values in JSDoc comments
- Document complex business logic and non-obvious code patterns
- Keep comments up to date when code changes
